==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|præsumptuous|presumptious|presumtious||archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|presumptuous}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|presumptieus}}, {{m|fro|presumpcieus}}, {{m|fro|presumptuos}}, from {{der|en|la|praesumptuosus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈzʌmp.tʃuː.əs/|/pɹəˈzʌmp.tjuː.əs/|qual3=informal|/pɹəˈzʌmp.ʃəs/}}<ref>{{R:Jespersen MEGoHP|I|12.41|347}}</ref>
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈzʌmpt͡ʃuəs/|/pɹiˈzʌmpt͡ʃuəs/|qual3=informal|/pɹəˈzʌmp.ʃəs/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-presumptuous.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Going beyond what is [[right]], [[proper]], or [[appropriate]] because of an excess of [[self-confidence]] or [[arrogance]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|act=III|scene=i|page=105|column=2|passage='''Preſumptuous''' Prieſt, this place cōmands my patiēce, / Or thou ſhould'ſt finde thou haſt dis-honor'd me.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Kyd Edward 3|act=V|scene=1|passage=Is this the proud '''presumtious''' Esquire of the North, / That would not yeeld his prisoner to my Queen}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|19|13|passage=Keepe back thy seruant also from '''presumptuous''' sinnes, let them not haue dominion ouer me: then shall I be vpright, and I shalbe innocent from the great transgression.}}
#* {{RQ:Eliot Middlemarch|book=I|chapter=III|volume=I|passage=Dorothea checked herself suddenly with self-rebuke for the '''presumptuous''' way in which she was reckoning on uncertain events, but she was spared any inward effort to change the direction of her thoughts by the appearance of a cantering horseman round a turning of the road.}}
#* {{RQ:James Europeans|chapter=X|passage=“Of course,” said Felix, “I suggest nothing; it would be very '''presumptuous''' in me to advise you. But I think there is no doubt about the fact.”}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Crossing|chapter=XIV|page=576|passage=The powers of the world were making ready to crush the '''presumptuous''' France of the Jacobins, and the France of King and Aristocracy would be restored.}}

